        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an air fryer healthier than deep frying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, because food cooks with a teaspoon or two of oil instead of being submerged in it, so it takes on far less fat. It does not make fried food a health food, and the results still depend on what you cook. Vegetables and lean proteins done this way are a clear improvement over deep-fried versions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to preheat an air fryer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manufacturers recommend a short preheat of a few minutes, and it helps food crisp from the start. Some models have a dedicated preheat function. For small or quick-cooking items it makes little difference, but for frozen foods and anything that should brown quickly, preheating gives noticeably more consistent results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use aluminum foil in an air fryer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, but check your manual. Foil must never block the airflow, cover the entire basket, or touch the heating element, and it needs to be weighed down by food so the fan cannot lift it. For tidier cleanup, perforated parchment liners made specifically for air fryers are generally the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are nonstick air fryer baskets safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Used as the manufacturer directs, they are widely considered safe for ordinary cooking. Avoid overheating an empty basket, avoid metal utensils that scratch the coating, and stop using a basket that flakes. Fumes from overheated nonstick can harm pet birds, so keep birds well away. Some brands offer ceramic or stainless alternatives.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an air fryer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single figure, and it depends on how often you cook and how you clean it. Owners commonly report several years of use, with the nonstick coating as the usual limiting factor rather than the fan or heater. Hand-washing, avoiding aerosols and checking that replacement parts exist all extend the life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for crisping, basket or oven-style?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A basket model generally crisps more aggressively because the air is concentrated in a small chamber. Oven-style units trade some of that intensity for racks, capacity and the ability to bake and toast. If crispness is your top priority, buy a basket; if flexibility matters more, an oven-style unit is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air fryers use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
